      "stop_reason": "end_turn",
      "tool_uses": [],
      "assistant_text": "The **Guarded Write Journal** page describes a mechanism for ensuring all-or-nothing semantics when writing files. Here\u0027s what it covers:\n\nAccording to [[concepts/write-journal]], the Guarded Write Journal works by:\n\n1. **Recording prior state**: Before the guarded tool executor overwrites any file, it records that file\u0027s previous content (or notes that it didn\u0027t exist) into an in-memory journal.\n\n2. **Rollback capability**: If the agent run fails \u2014 due to a turn cap being exceeded, an unrecoverable error, or all attempted writes being denied \u2014 the journal can roll back every recorded write in reverse order, restoring previous content or deleting newly created files.\n\n3. **Transaction semantics**: This approach provides all-or-nothing semantics for writes without requiring a database transaction; the journal itself serves as the transaction log, held in the process\u0027s memory for the duration of a single run.\n\nThe page also notes that the Query agent (the type of agent you\u0027re currently talking to) never actually uses the journal\u0027s rollback path, since it has no write tool \u2014 the journal exists in the shared runtime library but is only populated by agents that actually write files.",
